# Claude-Context — read this first

You are an agent working on the **AsterMind ELM integration** into the n-dx codebase.
Three teams work this repo in parallel. This file is the map. Read it fully before your first
tool call.

---

## 1. The project in one paragraph

n-dx is an AI dev toolkit (sourcevision / rex / hench) that currently pays a hosted LLM
(Claude, OpenAI, Gemini) for every inference. Our job is to replace those LLM dependencies with
free, local **ELMs** (Extreme Learning Machines) via `@astermind/astermind-community`. Not every
call can be replaced — classification-shaped calls can, open-ended generation mostly cannot.
Deciding which is which is half the work, and those decisions go in ADRs.

Prior art already in `main`: commit `43d6db51` adds the dependency and
`scripts/elm-hello-world.mjs`, a seeded smoke test that trains an ELM on file-path archetype
classification under Node. **Read that script before proposing anything** — it documents gotchas
that will otherwise cost you an hour:

- `charSet` is interpolated unescaped into a RegExp character class, so a literal `-` must come
  last or it forms an invalid range and throws.
- Text training requires `useTokenizer: true`.
- npm's latest is `3.0.0`; the repo pins `^3.0.0`. v4 is tagged on GitHub but unpublished and
  breaking. Do not chase v4.

## 2. Command structure

**Command is collective.** The three leads — Nolan, Jarrett, Thomas — hold it jointly. There is no
authority above them. One intern leads one team; that intern's agents are their team members, and
the lead is accountable for what their agents do.

What "collective" means concretely: anything **outward-facing or hard to reverse** needs a second
lead's explicit sign-off before it happens — merges to `origin/main`, anything reaching
`upstream`, dependency additions, anything destructive. Anything reversible and inside your own
scope is yours; you don't need permission to work. A two-way disagreement goes to the third lead.

