## 3. Folder layout

```
Claude-Context/
  claude-context-instruction   <- this file. The index. Always read first.
  Command-Structure            <- command, teams, doctrine, standing orders
  GITHUB-WORKFLOW.md           <- fork, branches, sync cadence, working concurrently
  NEW-AGENT.md                 <- how to set up (or revive) an agent
  OWNERSHIP.md                 <- path ownership + naming conventions
  IN-FLIGHT.md                 <- cross-team claim board + rolling sync
  CHARTER-TEMPLATE.md          <- copy this to create an agent
  SYNC-TEMPLATE.md             <- copy this to write a team sync
  ADR/                         <- decisions: WHY we are doing it this way
  IMPL/                        <- plans: HOW we will do it
  Nolan-Agents/                <- Team Nolan
  Jarrett-Agents/              <- Team Jarrett
  Thomas-Agents/               <- Team Thomas
```

Each team folder follows the same convention:

```
<Name>-Agents/
  README.md            team model + roster + scopes + seams
  <AGENT>.md           one charter per agent (its memory + session log)
  BACKLOG.md           team claim board — SOURCE OF TRUTH for status + claims
  Notes/               async inbox — anyone may write here; this team reads it
  syncs/               team syncs
```

**ADR** — *Why.* One decision per file, immutable once merged. If a decision changes, write a new
ADR that supersedes the old one; don't rewrite history. Template: [`ADR/ADR-TEMPLATE.md`](ADR/ADR-TEMPLATE.md).

**IMPL** — *How.* A plan derived from one or more ADRs: files to touch, order, tests, rollback.
Mutable — update as reality intrudes. Template: [`IMPL/IMPL-TEMPLATE.md`](IMPL/IMPL-TEMPLATE.md).

**`<AGENT>.md` charters** — *What actually happened.* Append-only session log. This is the
context-reclaim mechanism: a fresh agent reads its own charter and resumes without re-deriving
anything.

**`Notes/`** — *What one team needs from another.* Anyone may drop a note in any team's inbox.
This is how a seam gets crossed without a collision.

## 4. Naming — no numbered sequences

Two people minting `ADR-004` on separate branches is a conflict git cannot resolve. So everything
is dated and attributed instead:

| Artifact | Convention |
|---|---|
| ADR | `ADR-YYYY-MM-DD-<author>-<slug>.md` |
| IMPL | `IMPL-YYYY-MM-DD-<author>-<slug>.md` |
| Note | `NOTE-<from>-to-<to>-YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md` |
| Sync | `SYNC-<NNN>-YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md` (per-team, so `<NNN>` is safe) |
| Branch | `elm/<lead>/<short-topic>` — the lead's name, not the agent's |
| Backlog ID | `<TN\|TJ\|TT>-<agent initial><n>` |

Date first so directories sort chronologically; author second so two people can never produce the
same filename. **Reference an ADR or IMPL by its full filename** — there are no numbers.

## 5. Your operating loop

At the **start** of every session:

1. Read this file.
2. Read your own charter — `<Lead>-Agents/<YOUR-NAME>.md`. That is your memory.
3. **Read your team's `Notes/` inbox.** Someone may be blocked on you.
4. Read your team's `BACKLOG.md`, then `IN-FLIGHT.md` — know what's yours and what's hot.
5. Orient in git:
   ```bash
   git fetch --all --prune
   git branch -r --sort=-committerdate | head -20     # who's on what
   git branch --show-current                          # where am I standing
   git status
   ```

Before you **edit**: check `OWNERSHIP.md`. Not yours → send a note, don't edit.

At the **end** of every session (or before context runs out, whichever comes first):

1. Append a session entry to your charter.
2. Update your `BACKLOG.md` row and release any `IN-FLIGHT.md` claim.
3. Send any note you owe another team. **A blocker mentioned only in conversation is not a
   hand-off.**
4. `git push` — unpushed work is invisible and at risk.
5. Commit your logs too. An uncommitted log is a lost log.

**Starting or reviving an agent:** [`NEW-AGENT.md`](NEW-AGENT.md) has the process and the prompt.

## 6. Git in one paragraph

`origin` is our fork **`AsterMindAI/n-dx`** — this is where our work lands. `upstream` is the
parent **`en-dash-consulting/n-dx`**. Run `gh repo set-default AsterMindAI/n-dx` once per
worktree, or `gh pr create` may open a PR against the parent repo by accident. Work always on a
branch (`elm/<lead>/<topic>`), always in your own worktree, never on `main`. Pull `upstream/main`
into our `main` daily; rebase your branch on `origin/main` daily. Push your branch the day you
create it. Full detail, including what to do when two of you collide:
[`GITHUB-WORKFLOW.md`](GITHUB-WORKFLOW.md).

## 7. The rules that prevent collisions

Full versions in [`Command-Structure`](Command-Structure). The short list:

- **Never commit to `main`.** Merging to `origin/main` needs a second lead's sign-off.
- **One agent, one worktree.** `git worktree add ../n-dx-<name> -b elm/<lead>/<topic>`, then
  `pnpm install`. This is not style — see the next point.
- **PRD state has no locking.** `.rex/prd_tree/`, `.sourcevision/`, `.hench/` — concurrent writers
  lose data **silently**, no error, last writer wins. So `ndx plan|work|ci|refresh|self-heal` and
  rex MCP writes are safe in your own worktree, and must be **claimed in `IN-FLIGHT.md`** in a
  shared checkout. `ndx status` / `ndx usage` are read-only and always safe.
- **The ELM is a registered vendor, not a fork.** `ProviderRegistry.register(vendor, factory)` at
  `packages/llm-client/src/provider-registry.ts:96` is the seam. Bolting ELM into existing
  provider files guarantees three-way conflicts.
- **Claim before you start**, in your team's `BACKLOG.md`. Git is the lock; first commit wins.
- **Announce dependency additions** before `pnpm add` — `pnpm-lock.yaml` conflicts brutally.
  Resolve by re-running `pnpm install`, never by hand-merging.
- **Architecture tests are enforcement, not advice.** `pnpm typecheck && pnpm test` before you
  claim anything is done.
- **Changeset bumps default to `patch`** (root `CLAUDE.md`).

## 8. Honesty requirements

Your logs and ADRs are what other people use to decide what to build next. Therefore:

- **If tests fail, say so, with the output.** Do not write "done".
- **If you skipped part of a task, name the part and why.** A partial fix presented as complete is
  worse than an honest gap, because it closes the ticket.
- **An ELM that scores badly is a finding, not a failure** — record it in an ADR with the numbers.
  A measured "this call site can't be replaced" is worth more than a forced replacement that
  quietly degrades output.
- **Never report an accuracy number without its seed and its baseline.** Three classes at 40% is
  worse than guessing. If it isn't a committed, seeded script another team can run, it didn't
  happen.
- **A subagent's report is a lead, not a finding.** Verify at `file:line` before you publish it.
- **When you find that something you already published was wrong, correct it where it landed** —
  in the original, not only in your newest document.
